1
00:00:02,340 --> 00:00:04,937
When you have deeply nested code like this,

2
00:00:05,466 --> 00:00:07,614
it can be overwhelming if you look at this

3
00:00:07,711 --> 00:00:08,015
code.

4
00:00:08,753 --> 00:00:09,875
Where should you start?

5
00:00:10,516 --> 00:00:12,744
Well, you're going to see various ways of

6
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:15,101
slimming this down and making it cleaner

7
00:00:15,101 --> 00:00:17,393
throughout this module, but one very

8
00:00:17,506 --> 00:00:20,215
efficient tweak which you can make in a lot

9
00:00:20,215 --> 00:00:23,485
of cases is that you use so-called guards

10
00:00:23,822 --> 00:00:25,537
and that you fail fast.

11
00:00:26,146 --> 00:00:27,012
Now what does this mean?

12
00:00:27,910 --> 00:00:29,866
Take a look at this simple snippet.

13
00:00:30,411 --> 00:00:31,645
We have an if check here

14
00:00:32,238 --> 00:00:34,354
where we check whether an email includes an@

15
00:00:34,434 --> 00:00:36,903
sign, and if that's the case, we do a bunch

16
00:00:36,903 --> 00:00:38,907
of work inside of that if statement.

17
00:00:39,773 --> 00:00:41,921
And that bunch of work could be multiple

18
00:00:41,937 --> 00:00:44,550
lines of code. It could be as much as all

19
00:00:44,678 --> 00:00:47,003
the code you have here in the outermost if

20
00:00:47,003 --> 00:00:50,097
statement. So all this code here ultimately

21
00:00:50,193 --> 00:00:52,164
is in this one if check here.

22
00:00:53,574 --> 00:00:55,209
Now, if you have a case like this,

23
00:00:56,042 --> 00:00:57,901
you often can use a guard,

24
00:00:58,446 --> 00:01:01,362
which means you simply invert the if check.

25
00:01:01,826 --> 00:01:03,861
For example, in this case, by checking for

26
00:01:03,893 --> 00:01:06,185
the opposite with an exclamation mark. So

27
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:08,973
for the email not including the@ sign,

28
00:01:09,742 --> 00:01:11,745
and this is JavaScript, but of course you

29
00:01:11,745 --> 00:01:14,405
can do this in any programming language. The

30
00:01:14,405 --> 00:01:16,392
exact syntax might just matter.

31
00:01:17,049 --> 00:01:18,699
And then you do one important other thing.

32
00:01:19,437 --> 00:01:22,103
You just return inside of that if statement.

33
00:01:22,825 --> 00:01:25,170
And that means that you now use this if

34
00:01:25,218 --> 00:01:29,217
check as a guard. It runs before your main

35
00:01:29,313 --> 00:01:30,019
code runs.

36
00:01:30,598 --> 00:01:33,087
And if you make it into this if check, so if

37
00:01:33,087 --> 00:01:35,720
some condition is not met, in this case, if

38
00:01:35,752 --> 00:01:38,289
the email does not include an@ sign, then

39
00:01:38,562 --> 00:01:39,927
you fail fast,

40
00:01:40,875 --> 00:01:43,749
which means you return, which in the context

41
00:01:43,781 --> 00:01:46,303
of a function means that the code thereafter

42
00:01:46,736 --> 00:01:49,220
all the code after the if statement won't

43
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:49,796
execute.

44
00:01:51,062 --> 00:01:52,745
This is what makes this if statement a

45
00:01:52,745 --> 00:01:55,934
guard. It guards the rest of the code. If

46
00:01:55,966 --> 00:01:58,642
this condition is not met, because we fail

47
00:01:58,770 --> 00:02:01,670
fast, we avoid that the other code will run

48
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:01,847
at all.

49
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,196
And we had the same behavior on the left

50
00:02:05,196 --> 00:02:08,753
side, but there the to-be-executed code was

51
00:02:09,009 --> 00:02:11,076
inside of the if statement and therefore it

52
00:02:11,093 --> 00:02:14,074
was nested inside of it making it harder to

53
00:02:14,138 --> 00:02:14,331
read.

54
00:02:15,181 --> 00:02:17,828
A guard is typically way easier to read and

55
00:02:17,844 --> 00:02:18,614
to understand.

56
00:02:19,529 --> 00:02:21,663
And you cannot just use that if you have one

57
00:02:21,855 --> 00:02:23,732
if statement which wraps all your other

58
00:02:23,844 --> 00:02:26,508
code. You can also use a guard if you have a

59
00:02:26,508 --> 00:02:29,251
nested if statement in another if statement.

60
00:02:29,877 --> 00:02:32,331
For example, let's say here, hasPurchases.

61
00:02:33,005 --> 00:02:35,331
We could also outsource this and turn this

62
00:02:35,412 --> 00:02:38,524
into a guard where we fail fast so that we

63
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:40,690
then have 2 consecutive if statements

64
00:02:40,866 --> 00:02:42,663
instead of one deeply nested one.

65
00:02:43,761 --> 00:02:46,056
And this is a super efficient technique for

66
00:02:46,735 --> 00:02:48,965
removing unnecessary nesting.

67
00:02:49,498 --> 00:02:51,196
Now let's apply it to our example.
